---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const title = 'Shopify email app directory';
const desc = 'Browse 21 app destinations and build a shortlist around your store’s workflow, data and operating requirements.';
const apps = [{'slug': 'activecampaign', 'name': 'ActiveCampaign'}, {'slug': 'aftership-email', 'name': 'AfterShip Email'}, {'slug': 'attentive', 'name': 'Attentive'}, {'slug': 'brevo', 'name': 'Brevo'}, {'slug': 'drip', 'name': 'Drip'}, {'slug': 'firepush', 'name': 'Firepush'}, {'slug': 'justuno', 'name': 'Justuno'}, {'slug': 'klaviyo', 'name': 'Klaviyo'}, {'slug': 'mailchimp', 'name': 'Mailchimp'}, {'slug': 'marsello', 'name': 'Marsello'}, {'slug': 'omnisend', 'name': 'Omnisend'}, {'slug': 'postscript', 'name': 'Postscript'}, {'slug': 'privy', 'name': 'Privy'}, {'slug': 'recart', 'name': 'Recart'}, {'slug': 'rejoiner', 'name': 'Rejoiner'}, {'slug': 'sendlane', 'name': 'Sendlane'}, {'slug': 'sequenzy', 'name': 'Sequenzy'}, {'slug': 'shopify-email', 'name': 'Shopify Email'}, {'slug': 'smsbump', 'name': 'SMSBump'}, {'slug': 'tinyemail', 'name': 'tinyEmail'}, {'slug': 'yotpo-email-sms', 'name': 'Yotpo Email & SMS'}];
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Is directory order a ranking?', answer: 'No. Entries are alphabetical. A shortlist needs evidence for your required workflow, current plan and store setup; position here does not establish suitability.' },
  { question: 'Should I replace my current sender?', answer: 'First demonstrate the missing behavior in your current setup. Compare the effort to repair that gap with migration, training, integration and ongoing operating costs.' },
  { question: 'How should I compare quotes?', answer: 'Use the same projected contacts, sends, channels, destinations and support requirements. Record included allowances, overages, contract terms and the date of each quote.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path="/apps">
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path="/apps" type="WebPage" />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <p class="editorial-kicker">21 catalog destinations</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl leading-8">Start with the operation you need to change, then use the alphabetical directory to open the relevant app pages. This directory supplies no test scores, supplier winners or price estimates. Check material product claims in current supplier documentation before committing a shortlist.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Browse the catalog</h2>
    <ul class="mt-8 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
      {apps.map((app) => <li><a class="card block underline" href={`/apps/${app.slug}`}>{app.name}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><article class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Choose a route through the directory</h2>
    <p>For a broken customer journey, begin with the <a href="/use-cases">use-case directory</a>: it separates welcome promises, cart events, delivery education and lapse signals. Bring a specific event, expected message and stop condition to the app page. A broad feature label such as automation cannot establish that a queued message stops after a purchase.</p>
    <p>For a platform replacement, use the <a href="/guides/selection-framework">selection framework</a> to record mandatory requirements and then the <a href="/guides/migration">migration guide</a> to inspect transfer and cutover work. For a different business model, the <a href="/for">store-type guides</a> help identify constraints such as recipient identity, reorder cadence and fulfillment uncertainty.</p>
    <h2>Check the native baseline before adding a supplier</h2>
    <p>Shopify Messaging, formerly Shopify Email, includes marketing campaigns and automations. Its current <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging">official overview</a> and <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations">automation documentation</a> are a starting point for checking available behavior. Compare the setup available to your store with the specific missing requirement; an arbitrary store age or order count does not establish a need to migrate.</p>
    <h2>Build a shortlist with evidence slots</h2>
    <p>For each candidate record the requirement, plan or integration prerequisite, current documentation link, demonstration date, observed result and unresolved gap. Separate supplier documentation from behavior you have actually observed. Leave an untested requirement open rather than turning a sales demonstration into a passing result.</p>
    <p>Use the same fixtures across candidates: a subscriber who purchases before the next welcome message, a cart updated after entry, a refunded order, and a repeat event delivered twice. These are proposed evaluation cases. Record what happened and who can reproduce it, including the event fields and stop decision, before concluding that a platform meets the requirement.</p>
    <h2>Compare the whole operating cost</h2>
    <p>Request quotes against one volume sheet covering billable contacts, campaign and automation sends, channel destinations, seasonal peaks, integration charges and support. Add existing tools that would remain, implementation labor and contract commitments. Treat an allowance or introductory rate separately from the cost of the expected operating month.</p>
    <p>Keep cost and outcome evidence separate. Attributed revenue can overlap across senders and does not establish incremental profit. Use comparable purchase windows, returns and discount treatment when evaluating a proposed gain; do not count forecast lifetime revenue as money already earned.</p>
    <h2>Decide whether to consolidate or keep a specialist</h2>
    <p>Draw the current path from capture to audience storage, email, other channels, reviews and reporting. Mark which system owns permission, customer identity and each queue. Consolidation is useful only if the replacement preserves the behavior you need and removes work; keeping a specialist requires a clear interface and shared suppression.</p>
    <p>Before cutover, assign one active queue owner for each workflow and recipient cohort. Disable new entries in the incumbent, cancel or drain pending messages and deduplicate replayed events. Recheck permission and purchase state at send time. A trial comparison should not create duplicate live recovery messages.</p>
    <h2>Leave with a reviewable decision</h2>
    <p>Save the selected candidate, rejected alternatives, unresolved requirements, current quote and owner of the next check. If the current stack meets the requirements after a repair, record that outcome too. Revisit the decision when a concrete dependency or cost changes rather than treating directory position as a permanent recommendation.</p>
  </article></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="App directory questions" />
</BaseLayout>
